preConditionHook:
// THE ISLAND TOP AND ISLAND BOTTOM. An island top is a gap UP, a handful of
// bars, then a gap DOWN whose empty band does not overlap the first one, so the
// bars between are marooned with air on both sides. Its BOTTOM -- the high of
// the bar that gapped down -- becomes resistance, and the rule fires on the
// RETEST of that level; the island bottom is the mirror and fires the long.
//
// THE TWO GAPS: bar ii gapped above its predecessor (low[ii] > high[ii + 1]),
// and a later bar jj (jj < ii) gapped below its predecessor
// (high[jj] < low[jj + 1]). Both tests are STRICT, TrueGapBar's measured
// spelling -- a bar merely touching leaves no band.
//
// NON-OVERLAP is read as the two BANDS being disjoint: the down gap's floor at
// or above the up gap's ceiling, el_ge(high[jj], low[ii]). The merged
// pseudocode spelled it low[jj + 1] > high[ii], which compares one ISLAND bar's
// low with another ISLAND bar's high and so is not a statement about the gaps
// at all; the band form is what "a gap down whose range does not overlap the
// first gap" says, and it is what leaves the island isolated. Recorded here as
// the one construction this slice had to decide.
//
// THE RETEST IS A TOUCH, not a one-sided inequality: |high[0] - islandBottom|
// within toleranceTicks ticks, the tick list's standard "within N ticks" form.
// The merged pseudocode's high[0] <= islandBottom + tol alone would be true on
// every bar below the island and would make the rule a standing state rather
// than an event.
//
// WINDOW: jj runs over the last maxIslandBars bars and ii over the
// maxIslandBars bars before jj, so maxIslandBars bounds BOTH the island's own
// width and how long after the down gap the retest may come. No second input is
// promoted for the retest window -- Brooks gives one number ("within a few
// bars") and one is what the merge carried. Deepest bar touched is
// 2 * maxIslandBars + 1.
//
// THE SCANS USE A FOUND-FLAG GUARD rather than a break, because EasyLanguage
// has no loop break and the twins must run the same statements; the first match
// found is the MOST RECENT island, since jj ascends from 1. Both sides are
// computed in the hook: EL does not short-circuit.
//
// Kept apart from GapLevelRetest, which needs ONE gap and no pairing, and fires
// on a superset of these bars.
// THE TICK is EL's own MinMove / PriceScale, both VERIFIED and per-symbol
// constants for the whole run, written DIVISION-FIRST in both twins so the C++
// reads token for token against its EasyLanguage. Read once into a local.
//
// Scratch below is declared in localVariables, not at hook scope: the generator
// only prefixes declared locals, so a hook-scope declaration is a redefinition
// when this rule is placed twice in one strategy. Every one is fully assigned
// each bar before it is read.
tickSz = ctx.MinMove() / ctx.PriceScale();
foundTop = false;
islandBottom = 0.0;
for (jj = 1; jj <= (int)maxIslandBars; ++jj) {
    if (!foundTop && el_lt(high[jj], low[jj + 1])) {
        for (ii = jj + 1; ii <= jj + (int)maxIslandBars; ++ii) {
            if (!foundTop && el_gt(low[ii], high[ii + 1]) && el_ge(high[jj], low[ii])) {
                foundTop = true;
                islandBottom = high[jj];
            }
        }
    }
}
foundBot = false;
islandTop = 0.0;
for (jj = 1; jj <= (int)maxIslandBars; ++jj) {
    if (!foundBot && el_gt(low[jj], high[jj + 1])) {
        for (ii = jj + 1; ii <= jj + (int)maxIslandBars; ++ii) {
            if (!foundBot && el_lt(high[ii], low[ii + 1]) && el_le(low[jj], high[ii])) {
                foundBot = true;
                islandTop = low[jj];
            }
        }
    }
}
